Falsifies `kernel::arch::x86_64::amd_vi::tests::a_firmware_exclusion_range_is_cleared`.

Architecture: x86_64
Environment: NIFE_IOMMU=amd

The defect: `clear_exclusion_range` reads the two exclusion registers and reports them, and never
writes either. This is the driver as provisional milestone 767 (AMD-Vi hardening before the first
AMD boot) found it: `set_up` touched neither register, so a range firmware left with `ExEn` and
`Allow` stayed in force under the default deny. The struct field that makes `set_up` call the
function cannot catch this, because the call still happens; only the registers can.

Expected to fail: `assert_eq!(r64(u.base, EXCLUSION_LIMIT), 0, "the limit survived the clear")`.
Measured on patagonia 2026-10-05 (UTC), QEMU 11.1.1. The base-register assertion after it cannot
fail under QEMU, which drops writes to MMIO 0020h; the test's own comment says so.

diff --git a/kernel/src/arch/x86_64/amd_vi.rs b/kernel/src/arch/x86_64/amd_vi.rs
index 3e553b60b..f56767c7c 100644
--- a/kernel/src/arch/x86_64/amd_vi.rs
+++ b/kernel/src/arch/x86_64/amd_vi.rs
@@ -479,8 +479,6 @@ fn clear_exclusion_range(unit_base: u64) -> ExclusionFound {
         base: r64(unit_base, EXCLUSION_BASE),
         limit: r64(unit_base, EXCLUSION_LIMIT),
     };
-    w64(unit_base, EXCLUSION_BASE, 0);
-    w64(unit_base, EXCLUSION_LIMIT, 0);
     found
 }
 
